# Every lane a translation draws, and whether any flow node lands in it. One row per (filing,
# layer) that becomes a lane, carrying the verdict rather than only the occupied ones.
#
# The empty lane is the fifth wrong reading
#   It is the only one a correct drawing produces by being complete. The other four notes answer a
#   glyph that means something else in BPMN; this one answers the absence of a glyph, so no care in
#   the emitter can prevent it. A lane drawn empty reads as an idle part of the system, and
#   `rank/incidence_reach.sqlc` is why it is not: which layers this model connects is sparse, and
#   its figures are complete, so most lanes are rightly empty, and every one of them carries a
#   figure the notation cannot hold.
#
# The mechanism is already stated
#   `diagrams/lane_grain.sqlc` states it, and this is the population it implies: what the buffers do
#   explain is why a lane is empty, because `pm.slack` holds three rows per layer and every one
#   renders as `none`. A substitute is not a glyph.
#
# The verdict is a column, not a `WHERE`
#   That is the whole shape of this file. Keeping only the empty ones would make this a difference,
#   and `examples/soundness` rightly demands a law for one: a difference drops its own denominator,
#   so a reader cannot see whether a count of empty lanes is most of the lanes or all of them.
#   Carrying every lane with `occupied` beside it is `rank/layer_reach.sqlc`'s answer to the same
#   question, and `checks/roster.sqlc`'s reason for reporting a rule that examined nothing: the row
#   exists before the population does.
#
# Driven from the list of every layer
#   That is the allowed use and not the forbidden one. This is a question of which layers the lane
#   set reaches, so the list of every layer drives and the population is `LEFT JOIN`ed, as in
#   `rank/layer_reach.sqlc`. `diagrams/lane_membership.sqlc` reads `pm.layer` directly, because that
#   one is a membership test, where the list must not be passed on. Same two relations, opposite
#   roles, and `algebra/dimension_use.sqlc` is what tells them apart.
-- layers/every_layer.sqlc against diagrams/lane_membership.sqlc; every lane, occupied or not.
SELECT l.filing,
       l.layer,
       count(m.node_kind) > 0 AS occupied
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) l
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(diagrams/lane_membership.sqlc)
) m ON m.filing = l.filing AND m.layer = l.layer
GROUP BY l.filing, l.layer
